{
  "transcript": "Question, answer, tease. Here's the pattern that kills conversations. You ask, she answers. You ask, she answers. Question, answer. Question, answer. Like an interview. And it's boring. The tease is how you break it. The bridge from making conversation into flirting. Truth is, asking questions isn't your strongest move. It puts you in the interviewer's chair with her just handing over facts. And every, oh, nice, me too, is agreeing too much. Safe, needy, forgettable. But you'll ask questions anyway. So when you do, turn it to your advantage. The second she answers, don't reach for the next question. Tease the answer instead. Like this. She tells you she's a lawyer. Dead answer. Instead of, oh, cool. You go, a lawyer. I should have known by how much you like to argue. But here's the key. Don't just comment. Exaggerate. Coffee for breakfast? I bet you're a bit of a workaholic. It's just a flat logical comment. Coffee for breakfast, amphetamines for lunch, probably cocaine for dinner. That's a tease. It's so over the top, she knows you're kidding. That's exactly why you get away with it. That's the whole move. A little exaggeration, plus giving her a little bit of shit in a playful way. It's not a 100% nice either. That edge is the point. But there's a line. You sounded like you were lying just then isn't a tease. That's an accusation. The tease teases. It never actually stings. Here's the part most people miss. The best tease often isn't about what she said. It's about how she said it. You ask what she does for fun, she hesitates. I guess I like to read. Don't tease the reading, tease the hesitation. You don't even sound convinced. That's the higher gear, the delivery, the vibe around the answer, not the words. Why does it land? A tease is a quiet challenge, far more subtle than flat out asking, less cheesy, less try hard. Challenge her a little and she wants to rise to it, to prove you wrong, to impress you. It never feels like a tactic. It just feels like it happened. This goes well past dating. The person who can take a dead one word answer and spin something playful out of it, instead of marching through a checklist of questions, is the one a room warms to. A light tease reads as ease, an interview reads as nerves. Here's the drill. A few minutes a day, take any flat answer and tease it. Exaggerate one truest thing or aim at the vibe, keep it playful. Keep at it and it stops being something you reach for and becomes second nature. The comeback just shows up. So put in the reps. That's how a dead question turns into the thing she's trying to win instead of an interview that goes nowhere.",
